{
  "rules": [
    "Near-term (≈1–10 days): model is generally slightly too high in calm periods (typical +0.2 to +1.0 EUR), but misses sharp upside moves; when a rally starts it underpredicts the next few days’ acceleration.",
    "Mid-horizon (≈10–22 days): model tends to over-impose a dip/mean-reversion (forecasts slide into mid‑60s) while realized prices often hold firmer; apply an upward correction when the model projects a drawdown without a clear downtrend in the last ~5–10 days.",
    "Late horizon (≈23–30 days): strong systematic underprediction during breakout/rally regimes (often −2 to −8 EUR by days 27–30); add a regime-based uplift when spot is making higher highs or has recently broken above the prior 20–30d range.",
    "After upside spikes: model frequently assumes reversion too early (overshoots downward) while outcomes show persistence; delay mean-reversion—keep forecasts closer to the recent high plateau for ~1–2 weeks after a breakout.",
    "Trend/lag effect: forecasts appear to lag trend changes—too low late in rallies and too high early in pullbacks; incorporate a momentum term (e.g., last 5–10d slope) to tilt the whole path in the direction of the recent trend, with the tilt increasing with horizon.",
    "Volatility compression: forecast paths are too smooth versus realized (understate tails); widen the distribution / allow larger moves, especially beyond ~2–3 weeks."
  ]
}
